<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bren light machine gun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1935</span> · Also known as Bren gun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bren light machine gun was the standard light machine gun of the British and Commonwealth armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It was developed from the Czechoslovak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26">ZB vz. 26</a> and made under licence at the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield from 1937. Its name joins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, where the design originated, and Enfield.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bren-light-machine-gun.jpg" alt="Bren light machine gun" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert DuHamel (Rsduhamel) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bren_wog.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
